---
layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: gpa-calculator
title: "GPA Calculator - 4.0 Scale Cumulative & Semester GPA"
description: "Calculate high school and college GPA on a 4.0 scale with plus/minus toggles, credit hour weights, and multi-semester cumulative tracking."
permalink: /tools/gpa-calculator/
h1: "GPA Calculator"
lastReviewed: "2026-10-24"
howto:
  name: "How to Calculate Semester and Cumulative GPA Online"
  description: "Step-by-step instructions to calculate credit-weighted grade point averages on a standard 4.0 scale."
  steps:
    - name: "Select Grading Scale Options"
      text: "Toggle whether your institution includes plus and minus letter grades (e.g. A- = 3.7 vs solid A = 4.0)."
    - name: "Add Completed Terms and Courses"
      text: "Enter your course titles, letter grades earned, and credit hour values for each class."
    - name: "Include Prior Academic History (Optional)"
      text: "Input existing cumulative GPA and total earned credit hours to project your new overall cumulative standing."
faqs:
  - q: "How is GPA calculated on a 4.0 scale?"
    a: "GPA is calculated by multiplying each course's grade point value by its credit hours to find total grade points, summing those points across all classes, and dividing by total earned credit hours: GPA = Total Grade Points ÷ Total Credit Hours."
  - q: "What are the standard 4.0 scale grade points with plus and minus?"
    a: "On a standard plus/minus 4.0 scale: A+ = 4.0, A = 4.0, A- = 3.7, B+ = 3.3, B = 3.0, B- = 2.7, C+ = 2.3, C = 2.0, C- = 1.7, D+ = 1.3, D = 1.0, D- = 0.7, and F = 0.0."
  - q: "Do all colleges and high schools use the same grading scale?"
    a: "No. Grading scales vary significantly by institution. Some universities award 4.33 points for an A+, while others cap all A grades at 4.00 or do not use plus/minus distinctions at all. High schools often use weighted 5.0 scales for Honors and Advanced Placement (AP) coursework. Always verify with your institution's official registrar."
  - q: "How do credit hours affect my GPA?"
    a: "Credit hours act as mathematical multipliers. A 4-credit lab science or calculus course has twice the impact on your GPA as a 2-credit elective course. Earning an A in a 4-credit course provides 16 grade points, whereas an A in a 2-credit course provides 8 points."
  - q: "How do I calculate cumulative GPA from past semesters?"
    a: "Multiply your previous cumulative GPA by previous completed credit hours to find prior points. Add your new semester points, then divide by the new total credit hours: Cumulative GPA = (Prior Points + New Semester Points) ÷ (Prior Credits + New Credits)."
  - q: "Do Pass/Fail or Withdrawn (W) courses impact GPA?"
    a: "Standard Pass/Fail, Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory, and Withdrawn (W) notations do not carry grade points and are excluded from both total points and total GPA credit denominators."
  - q: "Is my personal transcript or GPA information stored?"
    a: "Never. All calculations run strictly client-side inside your browser memory. We do not store, track, or transmit any student data, course names, or grades."
sources:
  - label: "College Board: How to Convert Your GPA to a 4.0 Scale"
    url: "https://bigfuture.collegeboard.org"
  - label: "AACRAO: Academic Record and Transcript Evaluation Standards"
    url: "https://www.aacrao.org"
  - label: "US Dept of Education: Postsecondary Grading Metric Guidelines"
    url: "https://www.ed.gov"
quickAnswer: "GPA (Grade Point Average) on a standard 4.0 scale is calculated by multiplying each course grade's point value (e.g. A = 4.0, B = 3.0) by its course credit hours to obtain grade points, summing all grade points, and dividing by the total completed credit hours: GPA = Total Grade Points ÷ Total Credit Hours. Pass/fail classes and withdrawn credits are excluded."
workedExamples:
  - title: "Typical 4-Course College Semester (Plus/Minus Scale)"
    inputs:
      - "Calculus I (4 credits): A (4.0)"
      - "English Comp (3 credits): B+ (3.3)"
      - "General Chemistry (4 credits): B (3.0)"
      - "History (3 credits): A- (3.7)"
    steps:
      - "Calculus: 4 cr × 4.0 = 16.0 grade points"
      - "English: 3 cr × 3.3 = 9.9 grade points"
      - "Chemistry: 4 cr × 3.0 = 12.0 grade points"
      - "History: 3 cr × 3.7 = 11.1 grade points"
      - "Total points = 16.0 + 9.9 + 12.0 + 11.1 = 49.0 points"
      - "Total credits = 4 + 3 + 4 + 3 = 14 credits"
      - "Semester GPA = 49.0 ÷ 14 = 3.50"
    result: "3.50 Semester GPA (14 credits completed)"
  - title: "Standard 4.0 Scale Without Plus/Minus"
    inputs:
      - "Biology (4 credits): A (4.0)"
      - "Psychology (3 credits): B (3.0)"
      - "Art (3 credits): A (4.0)"
    steps:
      - "Total grade points = (4 × 4.0) + (3 × 3.0) + (3 × 4.0) = 16 + 9 + 12 = 37.0"
      - "Total credit hours = 4 + 3 + 3 = 10 credits"
      - "GPA = 37.0 ÷ 10 = 3.70"
    result: "3.70 GPA"
  - title: "Cumulative GPA Projection Across Semesters"
    inputs:
      - "Prior Cumulative GPA: 3.20 (45 credits)"
      - "Current Semester: 3.80 (15 credits)"
    steps:
      - "Prior grade points: 3.20 × 45 = 144.0 points"
      - "New semester grade points: 3.80 × 15 = 57.0 points"
      - "New total points: 144.0 + 57.0 = 201.0 points"
      - "New total credits: 45 + 15 = 60 credits"
      - "Cumulative GPA: 201.0 ÷ 60 = 3.35"
    result: "3.35 Cumulative GPA (raised by +0.15)"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Averaging Letter Grades Without Credit Weights"
    description: "Giving equal weight to a 4-credit organic chemistry course and a 1-credit seminar will give an inaccurate GPA. Always multiply each course by its credit hours."
  - title: "Averaging Semester GPAs Directly"
    description: "You cannot take the simple average of two semester GPAs unless both terms had the exact same number of completed credit hours. Always sum total grade points and divide by total credits."
  - title: "Assuming All Schools Use 4.0 Unweighted"
    description: "Many secondary schools use 5.0 scales for Honors/AP classes, and some colleges don't distinguish plus and minus grades. Use the toggle above to match your institution's policy."
formula:
  title: "GPA Formula"
  expression: "GPA = Total Points ÷ Total Credits"
  explanation: "Multiply each class's grade point (A = 4.0, B = 3.0, C = 2.0, D = 1.0, F = 0.0) by its course credit hours to get grade points, sum the points for all courses, and divide by the total credit hours."
